WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.835 --> 00:00:04.725
A machine works like a person: with eyes, a brain and hands.

2
00:00:05.506 --> 00:00:10.545
The eyes are sensors: is there a part, is the clamp closed, is the pressure reached?

3
00:00:11.584 --> 00:00:13.209
The brain is the PLC,

4
00:00:13.407 --> 00:00:15.230
the programmable logic controller:

5
00:00:15.392 --> 00:00:19.909
a small industrial computer in the cabinet that decides what happens next.

6
00:00:20.946 --> 00:00:24.824
The hands are actuators: they make something move, like a motor,

7
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:27.970
a valve or the air cylinder that pushes the clamp shut.

8
00:00:29.016 --> 00:00:34.055
Sensors sit on inputs of the PLC, actuators on outputs: together the I/O.

9
00:00:34.772 --> 00:00:38.255
Most are on or off, usually at 24 volts.

10
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:43.693
The PLC works in rounds, the scan cycle: read the inputs,

11
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:47.908
run the program from top to bottom, write the outputs, and again.

12
00:00:48.902 --> 00:00:52.594
One round usually takes a few to a few tens of milliseconds.

13
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:58.732
At 10 milliseconds, the PLC reads every input 100 times a second.

14
00:00:59.755 --> 00:01:02.321
The program is often written as a ladder diagram,

15
00:01:02.483 --> 00:01:06.964
a drawing with rungs: the conditions on the left, what should happen on the right.

16
00:01:08.141 --> 00:01:11.682
On this rung: is there a part, and is nobody pressing stop?

17
00:01:12.486 --> 00:01:14.843
Then the output close clamp switches on.

18
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.147
The operator talks to the machine through the HMI,

19
00:01:19.309 --> 00:01:24.500
the human-machine interface: the screen with the status, alarms and recipes.

20
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.736
The program runs in steps: wait for a part, clamp, check, press, release.

21
00:01:31.439 --> 00:01:35.654
Each step waits for its condition; an interlock works the same way.

22
00:01:36.716 --> 00:01:38.330
Now the station stops.

23
00:01:39.053 --> 00:01:42.920
The HMI says: step 3, waiting for clamp closed.

24
00:01:43.661 --> 00:01:46.308
The PLC is not slow; it is waiting.

25
00:01:47.323 --> 00:01:48.565
So look at the clamp.

26
00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:49.819
Closed?

27
00:01:50.598 --> 00:01:54.290
Then it is the sensor: shifted, dirty or a broken cable.

28
00:01:55.004 --> 00:01:55.399
Open?

29
00:01:56.152 --> 00:01:57.650
Then the air or the valve.

30
00:01:59.017 --> 00:02:04.114
The emergency stop, red on yellow, stops the machine: the power off at once,

31
00:02:04.276 --> 00:02:06.656
or braking first and then the power off.

32
00:02:07.646 --> 00:02:12.743
It is not wired to the ordinary PLC but with two wires to a safety relay,

33
00:02:12.836 --> 00:02:16.029
which drops out two contactors: a safety function.

34
00:02:17.035 --> 00:02:21.853
If a wire breaks, the relay drops out too: a fault makes the machine stop,

35
00:02:22.028 --> 00:02:23.061
not dangerous.

36
00:02:24.083 --> 00:02:29.006
It stays pressed in, and pulling it out starts nothing: first a deliberate reset,

37
00:02:29.226 --> 00:02:29.958
then start.

38
00:02:30.738 --> 00:02:33.571
An emergency stop never replaces the guarding.

39
00:02:34.882 --> 00:02:37.552
How reliable does such a function have to be?

40
00:02:38.232 --> 00:02:42.563
An operator opens the door of a robot cell about 10 times an hour.

41
00:02:43.256 --> 00:02:44.986
Door open, robot stops.

42
00:02:45.994 --> 00:02:49.303
That is the performance level, from PL a to PL e.

43
00:02:49.957 --> 00:02:54.729
The risk assessment asks: how severe, how often, and can you still get away?

44
00:02:55.696 --> 00:03:00.874
Severe injury; we read ten times an hour as frequent; the robot is slow here,

45
00:03:01.025 --> 00:03:02.662
so getting away is possible.

46
00:03:03.411 --> 00:03:04.758
That gives PL d.

47
00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.434
A fast robot: PL e.

48
00:03:08.418 --> 00:03:12.447
With the CE marking the maker declares that the machine meets the rules.

49
00:03:13.126 --> 00:03:19.860
From 20 January 2027 the Machinery Regulation replaces the Machinery Directive.

50
00:03:21.169 --> 00:03:24.815
Now a bottling line: six machines in a row, with the filler,

51
00:03:24.977 --> 00:03:26.324
the capper and the labeller.

52
00:03:27.332 --> 00:03:31.488
OEE is availability times performance times quality.

53
00:03:32.199 --> 00:03:38.851
Every machine reports above 90 percent availability, but the line OEE is 58 percent.

54
00:03:39.889 --> 00:03:43.407
The hidden loss: the micro-stop, seconds to minutes stopped,

55
00:03:43.592 --> 00:03:46.181
quickly reset and hardly ever written down.

56
00:03:47.205 --> 00:03:50.467
The team wants to replace the filler, the slowest on paper.

57
00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:51.660
Rightly?

58
00:03:52.946 --> 00:03:58.565
Every PLC logs its machine states: running, faulted, starved, waiting for supply,

59
00:03:58.751 --> 00:04:00.736
or blocked, unable to pass on.

60
00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:05.511
The filler runs 71 percent, 22 percent blocked.

61
00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:09.804
The capper 68 percent, 25 percent blocked.

62
00:04:10.569 --> 00:04:14.586
The labeller runs 74 percent, 23 percent faulted.

63
00:04:15.651 --> 00:04:19.215
The labeller hardly ever waits: it is the hidden bottleneck.

64
00:04:19.939 --> 00:04:22.458
A new filler would just be blocked more often.

65
00:04:23.512 --> 00:04:25.799
It logs about 40 stops an hour.

66
00:04:26.531 --> 00:04:30.652
Tackle those micro-stops, then look again: the bottleneck may move.

67
00:04:31.961 --> 00:04:33.412
Back to the press station.

68
00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:38.866
Every stop is in the alarm log: time, alarm code, text and duration.

69
00:04:39.844 --> 00:04:42.421
But one jam gives a whole chain of alarms.

70
00:04:43.452 --> 00:04:47.377
The first-out alarm is the first in that chain, usually the cause.

71
00:04:48.123 --> 00:04:52.164
Without the first-out, people chase symptoms, such as door open.

72
00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:59.083
A week of 10 shifts: part missing 1,200 times, servo fault 6 times,

73
00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:01.219
door opened 300 times.

74
00:05:02.547 --> 00:05:07.447
Put them in a Pareto chart, from largest to smallest, and make it twice.

75
00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:10.380
By count, part missing wins by far.

76
00:05:11.403 --> 00:05:16.605
By minutes: 1,200 times 20 seconds is 400 minutes;

77
00:05:16.744 --> 00:05:20.424
servo fault and door opened 150 each.

78
00:05:21.145 --> 00:05:22.538
It is on top here too.

79
00:05:23.569 --> 00:05:25.961
Check the first-out and look at the feeder.

80
00:05:26.704 --> 00:05:29.502
The door probably opens to free those parts.

81
00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:32.031
The servo goes to maintenance.

82
00:05:33.017 --> 00:05:39.054
An MES, a manufacturing execution system, links states and alarms to order,

83
00:05:39.309 --> 00:05:40.447
shift and reason.

84
00:05:41.143 --> 00:05:43.883
Go over the top three at the line every morning.
